body:
## Status consolidation

The Phase 3 decision package has served its purpose.

Owner decisions already recorded here remain in force:

- B+C integrated paper framing;
- the three-contribution structure at a high level;
- incident inclusion policy;
- title direction.

The remaining work is no longer a Phase 3 decision question. Final claim wording, numerical scope, causal-strength calibration, and related-work completion are tracked in #42. In particular, CLM-002–005 must be finalized only after the #42 follow-up.

Closing this issue as completed. Future revisions should proceed through #42 and the manuscript/review issues rather than reopening this decision package.

## Current role after the latest merges

This issue is now the main cross-cutting ledger for manuscript calibration.

Completed elsewhere:

- Phase 3 high-level framing and contribution selection: #35 (closed);
- author-interface evidence and analysis layer: #60/#66, merged via PR #68;
- CI follow-up: #67, merged via PR #69;
- first architecture-figure draft package: #58, merged via PR #72.

Remaining scope here:

1. complete the related-work baseline, including TauCeti, analysis/PDE formalizations, project-scale Lean work, and agentic theorem-proving/software-engineering comparisons;
2. absorb #71 as the concrete Lean Eval comparison task, while preserving the distinction between isolated theorem completion and repository-level formalization campaigns;
3. finalize CLM-002–005 scope and wording, especially purposive-sample limitations, causal-strength calibration, machine-wide versus attributed costs, and exclusion of post-campaign paper work from campaign totals;
4. decide whether the optional constructivity/computability discussion fits within the six-page limit.

This issue remains open and should be treated as the principal pre-submission calibration task.

## Progress update after PR #72

A draft artwork package has now been merged via PR #72:

- editable SVG sources;
- PDF and PNG previews;
- a design note and accessible descriptions;
- separate technical-harness and author-interface companion figures.

The original `Owner TODO: draw` item is therefore superseded. This issue remains open for finalization rather than initial drawing.

Remaining work:

1. owner selects, revises, or rejects the draft composition;
2. decide whether one or both figures enter the paper;
3. finalize caption and alternative text;
4. insert the approved figure(s) into the manuscript;
5. rebuild the six-page PDF and inspect legibility, grayscale behavior, spacing, and page balance at actual paper scale.

The merged files remain draft artwork and should not be treated as final approval.

## Status after PR #68

Wave B is complete and merged through PR #68. The repository now contains:

- the role and authority model;
- the passage-level audit;
- representative intervention traces, including drift cases;
- the deanonymization checklist and metadata switch;
- updated AI-use provenance.

This umbrella remains open only for residual decisions and Wave C integration.

Remaining items:

1. amend the INC-005 account so the 2026-07-18 06:30:55Z event is recorded as an observation followed by a decision not to act, not as human detection;
2. decide how the paper repository and reproducibility artifacts will be publicly identifiable and citable;
3. finalize submission-anonymity and public-reference policy;
4. settle the disclosure scope for ChatGPT-drafted owner-authority wording where needed;
5. transfer only confirmed owner decisions and justified manuscript/claim changes;
6. coordinate the optional export reconstruction in #70 without making it blocking.

The completed Wave B work should not be reopened unless new evidence contradicts it. Future work should be narrowly limited to the residual list above.

## Triage and integration path

This remains a concrete related-work task, but it should be executed as part of the broader baseline work in #42 rather than as a separate manuscript-editing lane.

Required output is intentionally small:

- verify the Lean Eval citation and benchmark description;
- add a concise comparison in the Introduction or Related Work;
- distinguish isolated theorem-completion problems from persistent repository-level formalization campaigns;
- avoid describing benchmark entries as complete reusable formalizations unless an individual submission has been audited to support that claim.

Recommended workflow: prepare the source/citation and wording under #71, integrate it in the same related-work pass as #42, then close #71 once the manuscript comparison is merged.

Current-head owner review at `f1ba621`.

The integration mechanics are now substantially cleaner: the out-of-scope CI selftest edits are gone, the provenance row deletion is repaired, the terminology is aligned to `scientific owner`, the in-figure title is removed, the overlap reported in the earlier review is no longer present in the current SVG patch, and both `checks` and `paper` workflows are green. The owner has also now explicitly requested figure insertion, so using the technical harness figure as the primary candidate is authorized.

One blocking presentation issue remains before merge: **the smallest figure text is still too small at actual IEEE scale**. The SVG continues to use 20 px layer labels and 22 px body/small text over a 1600 px-wide canvas. At `\textwidth`, this lands at roughly 6.5–7.1 pt. This is exactly the readability problem already identified by the owner: in the paper, the smallest text will be difficult to recover.

Please revise the artwork with paper-scale legibility as the primary constraint:

- remove or compress explanatory phrases inside boxes to a few words;
- use box labels alone where possible;
- shorten arrow labels to one word or split two words cleanly;
- increase the minimum effective text size materially, targeting at least the surrounding caption/body readability rather than preserving all current wording;
- rerender the SVG/PDF/PNG together and visually inspect the embedded figure at actual page size, not only at full-resolution source size.

Keep the technical figure only; the companion figure remains excluded. Do not close #58 until the revised paper-scale figure and its final caption/alt text are owner-reviewed. Also rebase after whichever of #74/#76 merges first, because `provenance/ai-use.md` overlaps.

Current-head owner review at `bc8a0a7`.

The evidence and wording corrections from the earlier review are now largely resolved: the INC-005 event is paraphrased without the unsupported causal clause, the connector claim is limited to the issue-creation channel and tied to repository snapshot evidence, the manuscript distinguishes the second status request, the formal-review channel uncertainty is preserved as an open question, `provenance/ai-use.md` is updated, the anonymity-switch comment is corrected, and both CI workflows are green.

One blocking operational issue remains: the PR sets `\anonymousfalse` as the default while the real byline and affiliation remain `TODO(owner)`. Consequently the default PDF is explicitly not submission-ready. The owner decision is that the eventual submission is non-blind; it does not require making a placeholder-bearing PDF the repository default before the byline exists.

Please separate policy from activation:

- keep the owner decision in `provenance/author-decisions.md`;
- retain the working anonymity switch;
- leave the distributable anonymous configuration as the temporary default until the real byline and affiliation are supplied, **or** supply the complete real metadata in this PR;
- state clearly in the PR body which configuration is currently submission-ready.

Do not close #60: the PR correctly uses `Refs #60`, and the umbrella still has owner-only publication and provenance decisions. Rebase and rerun both output modes after earlier overlapping PRs merge, especially because `provenance/ai-use.md` is changed by all four current PRs.
